AUDIO: 'Hi friends. Let\'s talk about the Gabo net. It\'s been a long time since I\'ve actually released some kind of app experiment or hands-on AI design. But only because I\'ve been sinking months into something more practical with the'

[00:20] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'help of a couple of friends. We\'ve been working on a one-stop shop for local role players and folks new to the local side of the AI scene with zero technical barriers for entry. Doesn\'t matter if'

[00:32] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'you want to use this for light coding, informative chat sessions, or running an agentic D&amp;D campaign, Gabo net handles most daily uses for LLMs outside of high-end coding tasks and complex'

[00:45] [scene:UI]
VISUAL: The GobboNet Setup wizard (version 1.3) is displayed, showing the installation process.
AUDIO: 'agentic workflows. Normally, this is the part of a demo video where someone goes on for 15 minutes about backstories before telling you the things that you actually need to know. So, let\'s not do that. If you\'re looking for information'

[00:59] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'on the bells and whistles though, go to this timestamp. If you just want to know how to set it up, here we go. It\'s about as simple as it gets. You go to this URL, download the EXE, run the installer. Windows might try and scream'

[01:12] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'at you here by the way. Hit show more and run anyways. We likely won\'t be getting a cert anytime soon because those cost money and literally just prove that we have a few hundred bucks'

[01:24] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'to spend. So, you pick a password and then a model from the suggested list and that is it. There\'s nothing to pull from get, no choosing a back end, no trying to weigh just how far you can push your current setup to run the best model'

[01:37] [scene:visual]
VISUAL: An animated character holds a blank paper.
AUDIO: 'possible or a mod to pull up just so that you can chat locally from your phone. Gabo net handles all of that for you and it does so without subscriptions, necessary API keys, an internet connection, or data collection.'

[01:50] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Not from me, not from anyone else. This was designed to be private and easy to use. It is only as censored as the model you choose and it can literally run on a decade-old laptop. But that ease of use'

[02:03] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'is the same reason it isn\'t a supplement for SillyTavern. SillyTavern lets you bring your own back end and with 3 years of incredibly confident devs behind it and a giant community making mods,'

[02:16] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'they\'re the go-to for a reason. I think of GaboNet like a stepping stone to get somebody to the point where they can comfortably [music] run applications like SillyTavern. That said, if you\'re using some kind of paid'

[02:30] [scene:visual]
VISUAL: An animated character gestures towards a chatbot icon.
AUDIO: 'online service that is like, "Give us some money and we\'re going to give you some uncensored AI." then yeah, GaboNet is going to do the job and then some. Character AI, Emochi, Chai, Replika,'

[02:42] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'PolyBuzz, SpicyChat, PolyAI, CandyAI, and I\'m sure a bunch of other things I\'m not listing here. You could ditch every one of these services for this app and'

[02:54] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'arguably you\'d have a better experience every time on the things that actually matter, you know, like talking with bots. So, let\'s jump into it. GaboNet was developed by three individuals. A'

[03:07] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'friend of the channel with a cybersecurity PhD helped shore up some of the pitfalls and John McArdle, the dude who made Ageless Linux, uh he has been helping us out with the porting'

[03:19] [scene:visual]
VISUAL: An animated character points, with text "I mean, yeah," on screen.
AUDIO: 'framework for Linux and Mac that we\'re hoping to release soon. I\'ve handled a lot of the rest, which to clear it up, yes, that does mean we\'re using AI for some of the code here, but everyone'

[03:33] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'working on this project has been working in their respective lane for years. This isn\'t even my first go-round with a front end. It\'s just the first one designed for local models. My hope is that between the three of us, we\'ve'

[03:47] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'managed to produce something useful and if someone out there is going, "That just means that this is slop." I mean, yeah, maybe. The rule in tech used to be eat your own dog food, and I hate that'

[03:59] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'phrase so much. So, with the industry now leaning into drink your own champagne, I\'m happy to apply that design philosophy from the bottom up. This is my go-to app for daily AI use. I use it all the time, and not because we'

[04:12] [scene:visual]
VISUAL: An animated character looks confused next to a flowchart illustrating "Long Content LLM Approach" and "Retrieval-Augmented Generation."
AUDIO: 'made it, but because as someone who has a stupid high standard for things like role-playing and has been refining that standard for two decades, uh things have to be comprehensive, and and they have'

[04:25] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'to be simple to use. Kobold AI Net is currently on version 1.5.1. It runs off a single installer. We don\'t bundle anything heavy into it. It calls for a llama.cpp download for the back'

[04:37] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'end, the Hugging Face hosted models to run the chats, and then the text compression model to help manage context windows for long conversations. They\'re all pulled directly from their'

[04:50] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'respective sites. Then a hardware probe runs to detect if you\'re running Nvidia, AMD, Vulcan, or something else, I guess. Anyways, anyways, once it\'s looked at that and your available VRAM, it does'

[05:02] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'some fancy math and chucks out a list of default models for you to pick from. As of releasing this video, we can run a total of seven different model families, and you\'ll see them all listed on'

[05:15] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'screen. Uh I\'m going to keep working on ironing out edge cases as time goes on and make this more uh readily accessible by different models. During your first run, you\'re going to be asked to put in'

[05:28] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'a minimum six-character password, not because I\'m being an ass, but because if you\'re running this at home and someone decides to open up a browser and reach the 127 IP, you\'re going to be in for a few awkward looks. Having that password'

[05:42] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'neatly stored away is the easiest solution and one you can always reverse. Beyond reinstalling, the GitHub has an explanation on resetting passwords, so let\'s not dwell on that. Basically, the'

[05:54] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'system is safe to run at home, but I wouldn\'t go popping this up at the local coffee shop and expect everything to stay private if somebody really, really, really wanted to peep at your activity. At this point, you\'ve got one last thing'

[06:08] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'that you can do before you start chatting. If you didn\'t check this box on the install or you can find the LAN launch option in the folder you downloaded everything to. Right-click'

[06:20] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'launch LAN, run it as administrator, and just like that, you\'ve now got a LAN connection set up, and if the address for it ever changes, Gabo [music] Net sends you an alert when you boot it up, you will see it right here, and now you'

[06:33] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'can chat from your phone. From this point, with or without that connection, the web page launches, and you could just start chatting. But before we do, let\'s check a couple of boxes. This'

[06:46] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'system is as private as I can make it. You can run everything offline from this point, and it\'ll never miss a check, a toggle, a call for anything. [music] There is one exception of this that'

[06:58] [scene:visual]
VISUAL: An animated character holds a blank paper and smiles.
AUDIO: 'isn\'t really an exception. There\'s another back-end similar to Llama called Ollama, and yes, it is exactly what it sounds like. It\'s just a derivative version of Llama CPP with some extra'

[07:11] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'knobs and bugs for flavor. One of y\'all pointed out to us that Ollama was in fact not a telemetry-free service in spite of their stance otherwise. They do really enjoy those gray areas, so of'

[07:23] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'course, we opted not to use it as the primary resource. Thank you to Nifty for that one. Still, using just Llama locked us into zero native search capabilities. Gabo Net fixes this by asking for an'

[07:37] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Ollama API key in the configuration settings here and actively using it for search-based tasks when you hit this little search button right here. But, we'

[07:49] [scene:visual]
VISUAL: An animated character looks confused next to a magnifying glass with a red cross.
AUDIO: 'don\'t stop there and take the extra step to strip out what data we can from going back to O llama. Obviously, if you want to run search, you need internet, you\'ll need an API key, and you\'ll need O'

[08:02] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'llama. But, uh it\'s entirely optional to do any of those things. And if you\'re asking why O llama, it\'s because this project has been in the works for nearly a year and llama\'s search support isn\'t'

[08:15] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'native. Admittedly, this is an example of the path of least resistance. Beyond that though, the bells and whistles are probably what you\'re expecting. A few default characters help lay out the'

[08:27] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'capabilities, DMs, storytellers, and informant character that\'s just there to explain everything about the application. You can download additional models, put them in the designated model folder, and then while Kobold AI net is'

[08:41] [scene:visual]
VISUAL: An animated character points with an angry expression.
AUDIO: 'running, you can hot swap between models depending on whatever task you\'re doing. Character cards are a must-have across any front end, and you can bring yours from, like, literally anywhere to here.'

[08:53] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'It\'ll take the standard imports, the JPEGs, PNGs, V2, V3, all of those character cards. And if you\'re importing something it\'s unfamiliar with, well, it won\'t just toss you a rejection notice.'

[09:05] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
